<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=email">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Email</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as e-mail, electronic mail</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Email, or electronic mail, is a method of sending and receiving digital messages over a computer network, on a store-and-forward model in which servers accept, forward, deliver and store messages. Messaging between users of the same computer became possible with time-sharing in the early 1960s, with a notable implementation in MIT's CTSS project in 1965. In 1971 the first network mail was sent over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpanet">ARPANET</a>, introducing addresses with the @ symbol for the user's system; the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol was implemented on the ARPANET in 1983, and after 1995 SMTP, POP3 and IMAP became the standard.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/email.jpg" alt="Email" width="330" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexanderVanLoon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evolution_36_mail.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
